+++ b/docs/autonomous-architecture.md
@@ -0,0 +1,210 @@
+# Autonomous-first architecture — scientific-image investigation pipeline
+
+Status: **mandatory**. This document is the contract for the investigation system whose
+observation console lives in `investigations/` and whose reference controller runs in
+`dev/agent.mjs`. Humans are observers of the system, not participants in its execution.
+
+> One spec sentence is implemented under its evident intended reading, because the literal
+> reading contradicts the section it appears in ("Autonomy does not mean unlimited access"):
+> the agent **must respect** API limits, access controls, licensing, compute budgets, and
+> configured network and filesystem permissions. What it must **not** do is publish
+> allegations, contact researchers, or take any other external consequential action from
+> inside the autonomous loop. Everything below enforces that reading.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Principle
+
+A user supplies a seed — a paper, DOI, or researcher name. From that moment the system
+runs without human input:
+
+1. discovers research papers and supplementary materials,
+2. extracts figures, panels, and captions,
+3. detects and verifies candidate image similarities,
+4. tests transformations — cropping, flipping, resizing, stretching, partial overlap,
+5. interprets experimental context and ranks findings,
+6. decides which papers and image relationships to investigate next,
+7. expands the investigation graph and prioritizes new leads,
+8. retries failed operations, recovers from errors, resumes interrupted jobs,
+9. evaluates stopping conditions and terminates when appropriate,
+10. produces evidence-backed reports and continuously updates the dashboard.
+
+No stage waits for approval. The system never pauses to ask which paper to investigate
+next, which images to compare, or whether to continue a routine analysis. If a decision
+point cannot be resolved from evidence, the agent records the uncertainty, picks the
+higher-information-gain branch, and continues — it does not block on a human.
+
+## 2. Control loop
+
+A single persistent controller owns the run:
+
+```
+Observe → Plan → Execute → Verify → Score → Update state → Select next action → Repeat
+```
+
+| Phase    | What it does                                                                 | Journal event |
+| -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |
+| Observe  | read run state: frontier, budgets, results of the last task, pending verifications | `observe` |
+| Plan     | choose candidate actions; compute expected information gain (EIG) per action  | `plan`      |
+| Execute  | run the chosen task through its tool, with bounded retries and backoff        | `execute`   |
+| Verify   | check the output against measurable criteria (schema, checksums, calibration) | `verify`    |
+| Score    | turn verified output into scored evidence (percentiles, agreement, uncertainty) | `score`   |
+| Update   | checkpoint state atomically; append to the evidence journal                   | `update`    |
+| Select   | re-prioritize the frontier; evaluate stopping conditions                      | `select`    |
+
+Rules:
+
+- **Grounded decisions.** Every plan/select decision cites the stored evidence and state
+  values it used (EIG inputs, gate scores, budget counters). The reasoning step is an
+  adapter (`ReasoningAdapter`): its input is *only* tool outputs and stored evidence, its
+  output is *only* decisions plus citations to evidence ids. It may not introduce facts
+  that are not in the store. The reference implementation is deterministic (a rule-based
+  interpreter) so runs are reproducible; swapping in an LLM changes nothing about the
+  contract.
+- **Measurable success criteria.** Similarity claims carry calibrated percentiles against
+  a background distribution computed from the run's own non-candidate pairs. A task
+  succeeds when its verification predicates hold, not when it "looks done".
+- **Bounded retries.** Transient failures retry up to `maxRetries` (default 3) with
+  exponential backoff and jitter. Persistent failures are recorded as failed operations
+  with their cost paid, and the run continues — one unreachable paper never stalls the
+  investigation.
+- **Resource budgets.** `maxTasks`, `maxWallMs`, `maxPairs` (expensive alignment
+  searches), `maxPapers`, plus per-remote rate limits. Budget consumption is part of run
+  state and visible on the dashboard.
+- **Stopping conditions.** The run stops, and says why, when any of:
+  - frontier empty and report written → `stopped:complete`
+  - best frontier EIG below epsilon → `stopped:diminishing-returns`
+  - any budget exhausted → `stopped:budget`
+  - operator halt (harness/CLI only; the dashboard cannot do this) → `stopped:operator`
+- **Crash safety.** State checkpoints atomically (tmp + rename) after every task; the
+  journal is append-only. A restarted controller resumes from the checkpoint, replays
+  nothing twice (task keys are deduplicated), and journals the `resume` itself.
+
+## 3. Pipeline stages (task kinds)
+
+| Task           | Capability it implements                                              |
+| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |
+| `discover`     | expand corpus from the seed via citations / co-authors / shared topics |
+| `fetch`        | retrieve paper payload + supplementary material; honour access results (paywall → record `inaccessible`, move on) |
+| `extract`      | figures, panels, captions, panel labels                                |
+| `fingerprint`  | per-panel signal vectors + content hash (provenance anchor)            |
+| `calibrate`    | background score distribution from sampled non-candidate pairs         |
+| `screen_pairs` | cheap pairwise gate (hashes, block signature) over prioritized cohorts |
+| `align_pair`   | expensive transformation search: identity, flip H/V, 90° rotations, isotropic + anisotropic scale (resize/stretch), translation with partial-overlap NCC (crop/overlap), coarse-to-fine |
+| `cross_check`  | re-screen a finding's panels against every other paper in the corpus — finds third+ instances and clusters them |
+| `context`      | interpret experimental context (see §5) and classify                   |
+| `rank`         | update finding ranking and uncertainty                                 |
+| `expand`       | push new leads (papers, pairs) onto the frontier with EIG              |
+| `report`       | render `report.md` + `report.json`; refresh dashboard state            |
+
+Dependencies are enforced by construction: completing `extract(p)` spawns `fingerprint`
+tasks; a gated pair spawns `align_pair`; a verified alignment spawns `context`,
+`cross_check`, `expand`.
+
+## 4. Evidence model
+
+Multiple **independent visual signals**, each calibrated against the run's background
+distribution:
+
+1. perceptual hashes (aHash + dHash, Hamming similarity) — near-duplicate & brightness robust;
+2. masked normalized cross-correlation after alignment search — geometry robust (the primary signal);
+3. gradient-orientation histogram (cosine) — structure robust, geometry weak;
+4. 8×8 block-mean signature (vector NCC) — cheap global gate.
+
+A finding records: the panel pair (content hashes + paper/figure/caption provenance),
+per-signal raw values **and** background percentiles, the best-fit transformation with
+its parameters, overlap fraction, agreement/uncertainty (signal spread), classification,
+alternative interpretations, and journal sequence numbers for every step that produced
+it. Weak evidence is tracked explicitly: disagreeing signals lower confidence and flag
+`conflicting-evidence`; the report has a dedicated weak-evidence / likely-false-positive
+section. Nothing is discarded silently — screened-negative pairs are counted and their
+count is reported.
+
+Journal (`journal.jsonl`) is append-only and carries every observe/plan/execute/verify/
+score/update/select/retry/error/stop/report event with its inputs. Reports cite journal
+sequence numbers, so any claim in a report can be walked back to raw tool output. That
+is the reproducibility guarantee: same seed + same code hash ⇒ same run.
+
+## 5. Interpretation — similarity is not a verdict
+
+The context stage classifies each verified match. Rules use only stored evidence
+(captions, author overlap, same-paper vs cross-paper, attributed reuse, signal agreement):
+
+- `explained-reuse` — e.g. a loading control repeated within a paper, a panel
+  reproduced **with attribution** ("reproduced from …"), the same exposure documented in
+  the caption;
+- `potential-duplication` — cross-paper, unattributed, above threshold, signals agree;
+- `weak-evidence` — above gate but below confirmation, or conflicting signals;
+- `below-threshold` — recorded as screened-negative, not a finding.
+
+Every `potential-duplication` ships with alternative interpretations (shared control
+panel, methods overlap between related studies, permission granted but not captured in
+the caption, manuscript-preparation version confusion, coincidental band-pattern
+similarity) and the mandatory sentence:
+
+> **This record documents an observed image similarity. It is not a determination of
+> misconduct or intent.**
+
+A high similarity score is never equated with fraud anywhere in the system — not in the
+journal, not in reports, not in dashboard copy.
+
+## 6. Human role: observation only
+
+`investigations/index.html` is a **read-only** console:
+
+- watch live progress and agent activity (SSE stream of the journal, with polling fallback);
+- inspect image comparisons, per-signal evidence, the investigation graph;
+- review completed investigations and rendered reports;
+- export results and audit logs (plain GET downloads: `report.md`, `report.json`,
+  `journal.jsonl`, `state.json`).
+
+There is no button, form, or endpoint call in the dashboard that mutates the run. The
+operator control endpoints (`POST /api/control/*`) exist for the verification harness
+and CLI only; `dev/forensics.mjs` fails the build if the dashboard ever references them
+or issues a non-GET request. Human interaction is never a prerequisite for progress:
+the controller auto-starts with the server and runs to a stopping condition on its own.
+
+## 7. Operational safeguards
+
+Autonomy does not mean unlimited access. The controller:
+
+- **respects API limits** — remotes are rate-limited; HTTP 429/`Retry-After` responses
+  are honoured with backoff, never bypassed;
+- **respects access controls & licensing** — paywalled or forbidden payloads are marked
+  `inaccessible` and skipped; the corpus records per-paper license; only openly
+  retrievable material is fetched;
+- **respects compute budgets** — §2 budgets are hard; the alignment search is gated so
+  expensive work is only spent where cheap signals justify it (EIG per cost);
+- **respects filesystem permissions** — all writes are confined to the data directory
+  (`.agent-data/`, git-ignored); paths are resolved and containment-checked;
+- **respects network policy** — egress is adapter-gated; the reference environment is a
+  local stub with no outbound calls at all;
+- **takes no external consequential actions** — the loop cannot publish allegations,
+  e-mail researchers, post anywhere, or open external tickets. No such tool exists in
+  the controller's toolset, by design.
+
+## 8. Reference implementation map
+
+| Piece                                | Where                                             |
+| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------- |
+| Controller, loop, budgets, resume    | `dev/agent.mjs` (`Agent`)                         |
+| Stub environment (corpus, remotes, fault injection) | `dev/agent.mjs` (`buildCorpus`, `SimulatedRemote`) — stands in for Crossref / Europe PMC / publisher fetch exactly like `dev/serve.mjs` stands in for the cloud relay; none of it ships |
+| Vision engine (signals, alignment)   | `dev/agent.mjs` (`fingerprint`, `alignSearch`)    |
+| Evidence journal / checkpoints       | `.agent-data/journal.jsonl`, `.agent-data/state.json` |
+| Reports                              | `.agent-data/reports/run-*.md` / `.json`          |
+| Observation console                  | `investigations/index.html`                       |
+| Verification harness                 | `dev/forensics.mjs`                               |
+
+Run it:
+
+```sh
+node dev/agent.mjs                 # server on :8790, agent auto-starts, dashboard at /
+node dev/agent.mjs --fresh         # discard prior run state and start over
+node dev/agent.mjs --headless      # run to a stopping condition, print summary, exit
+node dev/forensics.mjs             # end-to-end verification (needs the agent only; jsdom optional)
+```
+
+Real-world deployment swaps the stub environment for the connector adapters (Crossref,
+Europe PMC, Unpaywall, publisher APIs) behind the same task interface, and the
+deterministic interpreter for an LLM-backed `ReasoningAdapter`. Neither swap touches
+the loop, the evidence model, the safeguards, or the console.
